A||||4||William Federle||Duke timing cover leaks||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||01-16-2001||09:00 PM||||A month ago I replaced the timing cover seal on my 1984 Fiero in an attempt to stop a leak there. At the time I noticed a few little dings in the seal seat, but I just tapped the new one home, and it seemed to help for a while.<P>Then it started leaking again and I replaced it again. This time I noticed some dents in the side of the timing cover seal seat and so I tried to seal the sides up with RTV. I also noticed that the sleeve on the hub wasn't exactly round. <P>It's leaking again. I got the prices on a new timing cover ($16.70), a new hub ($51.52), and a new pulley ($38.06) from my local Pontiac dealer. <P>My question is - do I need to get a new pulley if I get a new hub? Do they come as sets to properly orient the timing mark to the hub keyway?<P>I'd appreciate any help that I can get on this.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||01-16-2001||09:47 PM||||I don't think you need a new pulley.  You should be able to bolt your old pulley onto a new hub with minimal mis-alignment.  Just note how the keyway and timing marks are positioned on the old hub and pulley before you disassemble them.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jerpro||01-16-2001||10:28 PM||||you don;t need a new pulley, but make sure the old one is in excellent condition. If it is a little worn or it has dents I would replace it. Better do everything now than have to deal with it later.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||01-17-2001||07:03 AM||||Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||ggarsk||01-17-2001||03:56 PM||||Willaim,the pulley will only go on the hub one way,so alignment is no problem. Good luck,I have the same problem,it's just to cold to work on it right now.||||reg||1
